Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.32,0:00:06.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Set {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} at zero point nine, and\Nlaunch two copies, one millionth apart.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.16,0:00:12.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}We know the state to six decimals.\NCan we predict the date of the next flip?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:16.14,0:00:18.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At first, the copies move as one.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.49,0:00:22.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But their gap nearly triples\Nevery eleven time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:22.27,0:00:26.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}After about a hundred and fifty,\Nthey disagree completely.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:27.31,0:00:31.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The average wait between crossings\Nis longer: over two hundred.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:31.46,0:00:35.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each extra decimal buys only\Nabout twenty-five more time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:35.61,0:00:40.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the forecast fades before the average\Nwait ends. Only the statistics survive.
